                    id: "topic_linux_1",
                    title: "Linux Fundamentals & Architecture",
                    order: 1,
                    estimatedMinutes: 120,
                    theoryContent: "\r\n# Linux Fundamentals & Architecture\r\n\r\n## Why Linux for DevOps?\r\n\r\nLinux is the **backbone of DevOps** - over 90% of cloud infrastructure runs on Linux.\r\n\r\n### Key Points:\r\n- 🐧 **Open Source** - Free, customizable, and community-driven\r\n- 🚀 **Performance** - Lightweight and efficient\r\n- 🔧 **Flexibility** - Highly configurable\r\n- 🌍 **Industry Standard** - Most servers run Linux\r\n- 🛠️ **DevOps Tools** - Built primarily for Linux\r\n\r\n## Linux Architecture\r\n\r\n```\r\n┌─────────────────────────────────┐\r\n│     Applications & Tools        │\r\n├─────────────────────────────────┤\r\n│        System Libraries         │\r\n│         (glibc, etc.)          │\r\n├─────────────────────────────────┤\r\n│         System Calls            │\r\n├─────────────────────────────────┤\r\n│      Linux Kernel (Core)        │\r\n│  - Process Management           │\r\n│  - Memory Management            │\r\n│  - File System                  │\r\n│  - Device Drivers               │\r\n│  - Network Stack                │\r\n├─────────────────────────────────┤\r\n│         Hardware                │\r\n└─────────────────────────────────┘\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Popular Linux Distributions for DevOps\r\n\r\n### 1. **Ubuntu/Debian**\r\n- ✅ User-friendly\r\n- ✅ Large community\r\n- ✅ Excellent package management (apt)\r\n- 🎯 Best for: Beginners, general purpose\r\n\r\n### 2. **CentOS/RHEL/Rocky Linux**\r\n- ✅ Enterprise-ready\r\n- ✅ Long-term support\r\n- ✅ Stable and secure\r\n- 🎯 Best for: Enterprise environments\r\n\r\n### 3. **Amazon Linux**\r\n- ✅ Optimized for AWS\r\n- ✅ Pre-configured for cloud\r\n- 🎯 Best for: AWS deployments\r\n\r\n### 4. **Alpine Linux**\r\n- ✅ Minimal size (~5MB)\r\n- ✅ Security-focused\r\n- 🎯 Best for: Docker containers\r\n\r\n## Linux File System Hierarchy\r\n\r\n```\r\n/                    # Root directory\r\n├── bin             # Essential user binaries\r\n├── boot            # Boot loader files\r\n├── dev             # Device files\r\n├── etc             # Configuration files\r\n├── home            # User home directories\r\n├── lib             # System libraries\r\n├── media           # Removable media mount points\r\n├── mnt             # Temporary mount points\r\n├── opt             # Optional software\r\n├── proc            # Process information (virtual)\r\n├── root            # Root user home directory\r\n├── sbin            # System binaries\r\n├── tmp             # Temporary files\r\n├── usr             # User programs\r\n│   ├── bin         # User binaries\r\n│   ├── lib         # User libraries\r\n│   └── local       # Locally installed software\r\n└── var             # Variable data (logs, databases)\r\n    ├── log         # Log files\r\n    └── www         # Web server files\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Important Directories:\r\n\r\n**📁 /etc** - Configuration files\r\n- `/etc/passwd` - User account info\r\n- `/etc/hosts` - Hostname to IP mapping\r\n- `/etc/ssh/` - SSH configuration\r\n- `/etc/nginx/` - Nginx configuration\r\n\r\n**📝 /var/log** - System logs\r\n- `/var/log/syslog` - System logs\r\n- `/var/log/auth.log` - Authentication logs\r\n- `/var/log/nginx/` - Nginx logs\r\n\r\n**🏠 /home** - User data\r\n- `/home/username/` - User's home directory\r\n\r\n**🔧 /usr** - User programs\r\n- `/usr/bin/` - User commands\r\n- `/usr/local/` - Locally compiled software\r\n\r\n## Essential Linux Concepts\r\n\r\n### 1. **Everything is a File**\r\n- Regular files\r\n- Directories (special files)\r\n- Devices (represented as files in /dev)\r\n- Sockets, pipes, links\r\n\r\n### 2. **Users and Permissions**\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# File permissions format: -rwxrwxrwx\r\n# - = file type\r\n# rwx = owner permissions (read, write, execute)\r\n# rwx = group permissions\r\n# rwx = others permissions\r\n\r\n# Example:\r\n-rw-r--r--  1 user group 1024 Dec 10 10:00 file.txt\r\n│││││││││\r\n│││││││└└─ Others: read only\r\n││││││└──── Group: read only\r\n│││└└└───── Owner: read, write\r\n││└────────── Number of hard links\r\n│└─────────── Owner\r\n└──────────── File type (- = regular file)\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 3. **Root User**\r\n- Superuser with unlimited privileges\r\n- UID = 0\r\n- Home directory: `/root`\r\n- ⚠️ Use with caution!\r\n\r\n### 4. **Process Management**\r\n- Every program runs as a process\r\n- Each process has a PID (Process ID)\r\n- Parent-child process relationships\r\n- Signals for process control\r\n\r\n## Key Commands Overview\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# System Information\r\nuname -a              # System information\r\nhostnamectl           # Hostname and OS info\r\nuptime                # System uptime\r\nfree -h               # Memory usage\r\ndf -h                 # Disk usage\r\n\r\n# File Operations\r\nls -la                # List files (detailed)\r\npwd                   # Print working directory\r\ncd /path              # Change directory\r\nmkdir directory       # Create directory\r\ncp source dest        # Copy files\r\nmv source dest        # Move/rename files\r\nrm file               # Remove files\r\n\r\n# User Management\r\nwhoami                # Current user\r\nid                    # User ID and groups\r\nsudo command          # Run as superuser\r\nsu - username         # Switch user\r\n\r\n# Process Management\r\nps aux                # List all processes\r\ntop                   # Real-time process monitoring\r\nhtop                  # Interactive process viewer\r\nkill PID              # Terminate process\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Important Best Practices\r\n\r\n### ✅ DO:\r\n- Use non-root user for daily tasks\r\n- Keep system updated regularly\r\n- Document changes and configurations\r\n- Use version control for configs\r\n- Implement proper backup strategies\r\n\r\n### ❌ DON'T:\r\n- Run everything as root\r\n- Ignore security updates\r\n- Disable firewall without reason\r\n- Store passwords in plain text\r\n- Delete system files without understanding\r\n\r\n## Key Takeaways\r\n\r\n> 🎯 **Master the basics** - Strong foundation is crucial\r\n\r\n> 📚 **Man pages are your friend** - `man command` for help\r\n\r\n> 🔧 **Practice in VMs** - Safe environment to learn\r\n\r\n> 🔄 **Learn by doing** - Hands-on practice is essential\r\n\r\n> 🛡️ **Security first** - Always consider security implications\r\n          ",
